From: Robin <robinei@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: Intel: atom: sst: save and restore the DSP firmware over hibernation

intel_sst_pm only has .suspend/.resume/.runtime_suspend. Hibernation uses .freeze, .thaw,
.poweroff and .restore, so the driver does nothing for it: the firmware is not saved, and after
the restore the driver still thinks the DSP runs its firmware while the DSP lost its power. Every
IPC message then times out ("Wait timed-out condition:0x0, msg_id:0x1 fw_state 0x3", "fw returned
err -16"), the stream never starts and applications block on the audio device. It only works when
the DSP happened to be runtime-suspended (state SST_RESET) at hibernation time.

Use the existing suspend/resume handlers for the hibernation callbacks too.

Seen on a GPD Win (Cherry Trail, cht-bsw-rt5645, kernel 7.2.9).
---
--- pa/sound/soc/intel/atom/sst/sst.c	2026-10-09 12:34:08.557429480 +0200
+++ pb/sound/soc/intel/atom/sst/sst.c	2026-10-09 12:34:08.570142023 +0200
@@ -574,6 +574,15 @@
 const struct dev_pm_ops intel_sst_pm = {
 	.suspend = intel_sst_suspend,
 	.resume = intel_sst_resume,
+	/*
+	 * Hibernation uses freeze/thaw/poweroff/restore, not suspend/resume. Without these the
+	 * firmware is neither saved nor restored: after the restore the driver still thinks the DSP
+	 * runs its firmware while the DSP lost its power, and every IPC times out.
+	 */
+	.freeze = intel_sst_suspend,
+	.thaw = intel_sst_resume,
+	.poweroff = intel_sst_suspend,
+	.restore = intel_sst_resume,
 	.runtime_suspend = intel_sst_runtime_suspend,
 };
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(intel_sst_pm);
